/* SEWA – Electric Booklet (Regulations for Electrical Installations) 2020. Transcribed from the page images of the supplied PDF
 * (its text layer is scrambled). pg = printed page number; f = PDF page number (image pages/sewa/p-NNN.jpg). Verify against the original before use. */
(D.real = D.real || []).push(
{ id: 'sewa20-3.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '3', sec: '3.1', cl: '3.1.1–3.1.4', pg: 32, f: 42, t: 'Electricity incoming supply', s: ['MDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'The nominal electricity supply Voltage from SEWA is 415 Volts-three phase and 240 Volts-single phase. The permissible variation from the nominal Voltage shall be kept within +10% and -6%. Cables shall be 5-wire including neutral and protective conductor for three phase, and 3-wire including neutral and protective conductor for single phase. The neutral is solidly earthed at SEWA’s substation and shall not normally be earthed elsewhere in the electrical installations. The nominal frequency shall be 50 Hz.', a: { supply: '415 V 3-ph / 240 V 1-ph, 50 Hz; +10% / -6%' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-3.2.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '3', sec: '3.2', cl: '3.2.1', pg: 32, f: 42, t: 'Voltage drop', s: ['Voltage Drop', 'Cable', 'Cable Sizing'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage', 'Power'],
  x: 'The maximum allowable voltage drop shall be 2.5% (from Main Distribution Board to Final Circuit) of the nominal supply voltage for any electrical installations connected directly to SEWA network. However, for electrical installation connected to private transformer it can be useful to apply values in stages in a system, and a popular value of 2.5% for sub mains.', a: { vd_light: '2.5% (MDB to final circuit)', vd_power: '2.5% (MDB to final circuit)' },
  e: 'In Sharjah the limit is 2.5% measured from the main distribution board to the final circuit – tighter than the 4% DEWA allows from the point of supply. For installations on a private transformer, 2.5% is also a popular value for sub-mains.', rec: 'If one design is used for both emirates, size cables to the stricter SEWA 2.5% limit and record the voltage-drop schedule from MDB to the final circuit.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-3.3.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '3', sec: '3.3', cl: '3.3.1', pg: 32, f: 42, t: 'Prospective fault current – switchboard short-circuit rating', s: ['Short Circuit', 'Circuit Breaker', 'Transformer'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Transformer', 'Electrical'], x: 'The maximum three phase prospective fault current at the bus bar in Low Voltage side depends on the Apparent Power Rating in the substation to be installed. Short circuit rating of switchboard at 1 second: 100 kVA – 35 kA or 26 MVA; 200 kVA – 35 kA or 26 MVA; 250 kVA – 35 kA or 26 MVA; 500 kVA – 50 kA or 36 MVA; 1,000 kVA – 65 kA or 47 MVA; 1,500 kVA – 65 kA or 47 MVA; 2,000 kVA – 65 kA or 47 MVA.', a: { sc_rating: '35 kA (≤250 kVA) / 50 kA (500 kVA) / 65 kA (1000–2000 kVA)' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-4.1.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '4', sec: '4.1', cl: '4.1.1–4.1.9', pg: 38, f: 48, t: 'Sub-station / transformer room – general requirements', s: ['Transformer', 'MDB'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'SEWA shall be responsible for the requirements of sub-station/transformer room for provision of supplies to any new project or development. The substation/transformer room will be constructed as per drawings approved by SEWA. SEWA will provide padlock for the door of sub-station/transformer room. Sub-station/transformer room shall be free from gas lines and it shall not be located on the adjacent side of a bathroom, toilet, kitchen, pump room and etc., or below a bathroom, toilet, laundry and kitchen. It shall not be located below any water services or pipes such as mains of water supply, drainage systems, storage tanks, air conditioning chillers or other liquids or hazardous materials. The location, design and layout of sub-station/transformer room shall be approved by SEWA before construction of the building. SEWA MV cables should not pass in private plot but it is restricted to pass only in building setbacks or service corridor designated by SEWA. Crossing of MV and LV cables inside sub-station/transformer room are not allowed.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-4.1.10', d: 'sewa20', ch: '4', sec: '4.1', cl: '4.1.10–4.1.21', pg: 39, f: 49, t: 'Transformer room – single transformer: size, height, doors, levels, trenches', s: ['Transformer'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], fig: 'transroom',
  x: 'For (1) Single Transformer Equipment: The minimum dimension size of sub-station/transformer room shall be 4.5 m x 4.5 m clear. Sub-station/transformer room shall be RCC structure with RCC roof slab. Considering the nearest municipality road level to sub-station/transformer as 0.00 level, clear height of sub-station/transformer room or MV room (if any) shall be 3.5 m taken from the finished floor level of transformer equipment base to roof bottom slab. Minimum side distance between transformer equipment base and any structural members to be maintained at 0.90 meters. Sub-station/transformer room door opening shall be directly facing to road, sikka or side setback of 3 m. Sub-station/transformer room door shall be heavy duty aluminum louver doors with all-drop and to have a minimum dimension of 2.4 m x 2.4 m and to open towards outside. Finished floor level of sub-station/transformer room shall be at +45 cm from the road level (0.00 level) and sub-station/transformer room trench level shall be -45 cm from road level, maintaining a trench total depth of 90 cm. All trenches for cable shall be at minimum of 50 cm in width and free from any tie beams, columns and services. Three (3) numbers of 6" diameter heavy duty PVC pipe sleeves OR 40 cm x 30 cm opening shall be provided for MV cable access at -45 cm trench level. A 40 cm x 30 cm opening shall be provided for LV cables access at -45 cm trench level.', a: { tx_room_size: '4.5 m × 4.5 m clear (single transformer)', tx_room_height: '3.5 m (transformer base FFL to roof slab soffit)', tx_ffl: '+0.45 m above road level; trench −0.45 m (total 0.90 m)' },
  e: 'A single-transformer SEWA substation needs at least 4.5 × 4.5 m clear, 3.5 m clear height, a 2.4 × 2.4 m outward-opening louvre door facing the road, floor +45 cm above road level and a 90 cm deep cable trench.', rec: 'Dimension the room from the transformer base (not the slab), keep 0.90 m between the equipment base and any structural member, and get SEWA approval of the layout before construction.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-4.1.22', d: 'sewa20', ch: '4', sec: '4.1', cl: '4.1.22–4.1.34', pg: 40, f: 50, t: 'Transformer room – cable access, attic slab, clear height, basements', s: ['Transformer'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'Provision of main MV cable access 40 cm x 30 cm or 3 numbers of 150 sq.mm sleeves/coring in retaining wall of basement at -45 cm level. All steel columns or structural members inside sub-station/transformer room shall be provided with concrete protective casing. Attic slab with 60 cm clear void height with drainage shaft and water proofing shall be provided if any wet areas or drainage system on top of sub-station/transformer room. Chequered plates, steel angles or channels shall not be provided inside sub-station/transformer room unless SEWA requests to do so. Clear height of sub-station/transformer room shall be 3 meters minimum from finished floor level in case of attic slab or basement beneath it. In case of cable tray passing at high level of basement, no cable joints allowed along the length of the cables. MV room and main LV room must be adjacent and directly connected to transformer. All sub-station/transformer room doors, MV room doors shall be at finished floor level of base and no gate allowed obstructing its access.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-4.1.35', d: 'sewa20', ch: '4', sec: '4.1', cl: '4.1.35–4.1.42', pg: 41, f: 51, t: 'Transformer room – double and three or more transformers', s: ['Transformer', 'RMU'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'For (2) Double Transformer Equipment: The minimum dimension size of sub-station/transformer room shall be 8 m x 5 m clear. 1.8 meters distance shall be maintained between two transformer equipment bases. Two (2) numbers of heavy duty aluminum louver doors with all drop and a minimum dimension of 2.4 m x 2.4 m shall be provided. Two (2) numbers of LV cable access to be provided for each transformer. For Three (3) or More Transformer Equipment: a separate attached MV room shall be provided with 3 m x 2.7 m minimum non-louver aluminum panel door facing road, sikka, side setback and shall be fully air-conditioned. The required width of MV room shall be 5.5 m minimum and the length depends on the 11 kV/0.415 kV bill of quantities (BOQ) and single line diagram issued and approved by SEWA network study department.', a: { tx_room_size_2tx: '8 m × 5 m clear (two transformers)' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-4.2.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '4', sec: '4.2', cl: '4.2.1–4.2.3', pg: 42, f: 52, t: 'Low voltage switch room – minimum size', s: ['MDB', 'DB', 'SMDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'],
  x: 'The minimum size of low voltage switch room as per the main circuit breaker ratings are as follows: 1,600–2,500 A – 4.5 m x 4.0 m; 1,000–1,200 A – 4.5 m x 3.5 m; 600–800 A – 4.5 m x 3.0 m; 400–500 A – 3.0 m x 2.0 m; 200–300 A – 2.0 m x 2.0 m; 100–160 A – 1.5 m x 1.5 m; 60–80 A – 1.2 m x 1.0 m. [Note: the above room sizes are given for guidance only and other requirements for access (e.g. panel layout, sizes, dimension and spacing) and safety should be taken into account. Provision for capacitor bank & size allocation to be checked and consider.] Main low voltage switch room shall be adjacent and directly attached to one side of sub-station/transformer room.', a: { lv_room_size: '4.5×4.0 m (1600–2500 A); 4.5×3.5 m (1000–1200 A); 4.5×3.0 m (600–800 A); 3.0×2.0 m (400–500 A); 2.0×2.0 m (200–300 A)' },
  e: 'SEWA gives guidance sizes for the LV switch room by the rating of the main breaker, from 1.2 × 1.0 m (60–80 A) up to 4.5 × 4.0 m (1,600–2,500 A). The room must be next to the transformer room, and space for a capacitor bank must be checked.', rec: 'Start from the table for the incomer rating, then check panel layout, access and the capacitor bank space; these sizes are guidance only.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-4.2.4', d: 'sewa20', ch: '4', sec: '4.2', cl: '4.2.4–4.2.12', pg: 43, f: 53, t: 'Low voltage switch room – access, ventilation, location', s: ['MDB', 'DB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'The main door of the low voltage switch room will be of sufficient size to allow removal of the switchboard in the form of individual cubicle units. All low voltage main switch rooms shall be well ventilated, preferably without the need of forced air circulation. Where air conditioning is required, the requirement for fresh air circulation must also be provided to avoid condensation. [Note: consideration must be given to the relevant UAE fire code requirements.] Low voltage switch room shall be positioned in an area which is readily accessible to SEWA staff and other authorized persons particularly in the event of emergency and must be at or close to the outside perimeter of the premise. Low voltage switch room shall not be positioned in an area controlled by one of the tenants in a multi-occupancy building. Other than in exceptional circumstances, and with prior approval from SEWA, there shall be only one low voltage switch room for any premises. The use of low voltage switch rooms as storage room for any tools, equipment or other materials is prohibited. The low voltage switch room shall not be located on the reverse side of a bathroom, toilet, kitchen, pump room and etc., or below a bathroom, toilet, laundry and kitchen. It shall not be located below any water services or pipes.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-4.2.13', d: 'sewa20', ch: '4', sec: '4.2', cl: '4.1.13–4.1.18 (as numbered in the booklet)', pg: 44, f: 54, t: 'Low voltage switch room – emergency lighting, doors, multistorey rooms', s: ['Emergency Lighting', 'MDB', 'DB'], k: ['Lighting', 'Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], fig: 'emlayout',
  x: 'At least one emergency lighting unit shall be fitted in all low voltage switch rooms which must be provided with a battery rated for minimum 3 hours illumination and subject to adequate routine maintenance. Doors of low voltage switch rooms shall be arranged to open outwards, be kept free from obstructions and be capable of being opened from the inside without the use of a key. The need for delivery of heavy equipment to the low voltage switch room during construction and for future repair or alterations shall be taken into account in the location of low voltage switch room. For a low voltage switch rooms greater than 6 meters in length, more than one door shall be provided as a means of emergency access. For a multistorey building (G+1 and above), typical low voltage switch room shall be provided to accommodate the electrical equipment such as sub-main distribution boards, kWHr meters and etc.', a: { duration: '180 min', location: 'At least one unit in every LV switch room', el_standard: 'BS 5266 (clause 6.1.26)' },
  e: 'Every low voltage switch room needs at least one emergency lighting unit with a battery rated for a minimum of 3 hours. Switch room doors open outwards and can be opened from inside without a key; rooms longer than 6 m need more than one door.', rec: 'Show the emergency luminaire inside each LV room on the lighting layout and state the 3-hour battery rating on the schedule.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-5.2', d: 'sewa20', ch: '5', sec: '5.2', cl: '5.2.1–5.2.12', pg: 46, f: 56, t: 'Overload and short-circuit protection – breakers and ratings', s: ['Circuit Breaker', 'Protection', 'Short Circuit'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'],
  x: 'The main circuit breaker at the connection point shall be MCCB or ACB type and adequately rated for the maximum prospective fault current. All circuits shall be individually protected against overloads and short circuits by suitable devices. Replaceable or re-wireable fuse links are not permitted. Protective devices at the main distribution board shall have a prospective fault current withstand and interruption rating above the maximum prospective fault current declared by SEWA. To ensure discrimination of circuit breakers on individual circuits, the rating of the main circuit breaker is to be at least one step higher than that of the largest circuit breaker controlling an outgoing circuit. All circuit breakers of any type shall have a de-rating factor applied to their manufactured current rating, unless calibrated by the manufacturer for 55 °C; the de-rating factor shall be 80% of the current rating stated by the manufacturer. Miniature circuit breaker shall not be installed for main or sub main cable distribution. The standard circuit breaker ratings are: 6A, 10A, 16A, 20A, 25A, 30A/32A, 40A, 60A/63A, 80A, 100A, 125A, 160A, 200A, 225A, 250A, 300A, 350A, 400A, 600A/630A, 800A, 1000A, 1250A, 1600A, 2500A and 3200A. Circuit breaker used in an electrical installations of more than 1KA shall be Air Circuit Breaker (draw-out type).', a: { breaker_derating: '80% of rating unless calibrated for 55 °C' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-5.3', d: 'sewa20', ch: '5', sec: '5.3', cl: '5.3.1–5.3.4', pg: 48, f: 58, t: 'Electric shock protection', s: ['Protection', 'RCD'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Protection of persons against the risk of direct contact with electricity shall be provided by either physically preventing contact or by an inherently safe systems of supply: insulated conductors, secure enclosures/barriers/cover on live parts, SELV system, PELV system. Residual current devices with a residual current rating of 30mA and complying with BS EN 61008 and BS IEC 61008 may be used as a means of supplementary protection against direct contact. RCD devices with a residual rating above 30mA are not considered to provide adequate protection against direct contact. Protection against indirect contact shall be provided by an earth leakage protected system and an earthed equipotential bonded system. Short circuit protection devices provide the primary means of clearance of earth faults within 0.4 seconds.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-5.4.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '5', sec: '5.4', cl: '5.4.1–5.4.7', pg: 49, f: 59, t: 'Earth leakage protected systems – RCD requirements', s: ['Protection', 'RCD', 'Socket Outlets'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical', 'Power'],
  x: 'An earth leakage protected system (ELPS) is defined as one where protection against indirect contact is provided by the use of RCD’s or other similar devices on all final circuits and ELP is provided at the low voltage switch room. For final circuits, ELP devices must be of the RCD type; voltage-operated earth leakage devices (ELCB) are not permitted. RCD devices for final circuits must have a time-current performance characteristic complying with BS EN 61008: operate within 200 milliseconds at its residual current rating and within 40 milliseconds at 5 times its residual current rating; shall not operate below 50% of its residual current rating. Earth leakage protection provided at the low voltage switch room must be set to discriminate with RCD’s at final circuits. For domestic premises, the residual current rating for RCD’s must be no greater than 100mA for final circuits supplying fixed equipment (e.g. lighting and air conditioning) and no greater than 30mA for final circuits where appliances may be used by persons (e.g. all socket outlets, all kitchen appliances and other appliances accessible by persons) and no greater than 30mA for all circuits in a bath room. Final circuits with high earth leakage currents (e.g. electronic equipment or industrial machinery) may be provided with ELP device with higher residual current ratings up to 500mA.', a: { rcd_sockets: '30 mA', rcd_fixed: '100 mA (fixed equipment, lighting, A/C – domestic)' },
  e: 'In SEWA installations all socket outlets and appliances accessible to people are protected by RCDs of no more than 30 mA; fixed equipment such as lighting and air conditioning may use up to 100 mA. Voltage-operated ELCBs are not permitted on final circuits.', rec: 'Use RCBOs/RCDs (not voltage-operated ELCBs) on final circuits, 30 mA for sockets and appliances, and check discrimination with the earth-leakage protection at the LV switch room.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-5.4.9', d: 'sewa20', ch: '5', sec: '5.4', cl: '5.4.8–5.4.9', pg: 50, f: 60, t: 'Recommended RCD/ELCB operating current by circuit type', s: ['Protection', 'RCD', 'Socket Outlets', 'Lighting'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical', 'Power'], x: 'Special circuits where tripping of earth leakage protection would be significantly detrimental (e.g. data centers, fire protection equipment, safety alarms) may be excluded from the zone of earth leakage; such cases must be declared on the electrical installation certificate and approved by SEWA. Recommended rated operating current for ELCB/RCCB in consumer installations (mA): 13A switched socket outlet 30; water heater/cooler 30; refrigerator/washing machine 30; domestic pumps 100; under water lights/jacuzzi 10; 15A switched socket outlet 30; general lighting 100; flood lighting 30/100; window/split air conditioning 100/300; fan coil unit/AHU/VAV 100; package type A/C unit 100/300; chiller 100/500/1000; irrigation pump 100; electric cooker 30; industrial machine 100/300; elevator/escalator 300/500; neon sign 300; fire fighting – ELS + ALARM.', a: { rcd_lighting: '100 mA (general lighting)' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.1.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.1', cl: '6.1.1–6.1.9', pg: 58, f: 68, t: 'Lighting circuits – loading, cable sizes, controls', s: ['Indoor Lighting', 'Lighting', 'Lighting Controls', 'Cable Sizing'], k: ['Lighting'],
  x: 'Lighting Circuits will normally be fed from 6A, 10A, 20A MCB’s from a Final Distribution Board. All lighting circuits shall be installed with a maximum loading of 1,800 watts per circuit. Circuit breaker 6 A – phase and earth conductor 1.5 mm² – circuit max. load 600 W; 10 A – 2.5 mm² – 1,200 W; 20 A – 4 mm² – 1,800 W. LED type lighting fixture shall be used in all electrical installations except those special electrical installations that requires lighting fixtures other than LED type. All internal and external lighting points shall be installed on their own separate circuit/s or controls. Occupancy Lighting Controls such as sensors shall be used in corridors, lobbies, parkings, stores, open offices and similar locations. Lighting Control Circuits or timers shall be used for external lightings, decorative and chandelier lightings. Lighting Illumination (Lux Level) shall be reduced in the corridors and other areas that can benefit from natural light. Proper Illumination calculation shall be applied to all parts of installation to determine the lux level as per standards and the number of luminaires as well (e.g. DIALUX Software). Spot lights and general lights shall be in separate circuit.', a: { lighting_circuit_max: '1,800 W (20 A / 4 mm²)', conductor_light: '1.5 mm² (6 A), 2.5 mm² (10 A), 4 mm² (20 A)', lux_calc: 'Illumination calculation required (e.g. DIALux)' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.1.10', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.1', cl: '6.1.10–6.1.19', pg: 59, f: 69, t: 'Lighting circuits – protection, switching, luminaire connection', s: ['Lighting', 'Indoor Lighting'], k: ['Lighting'], x: 'Lighting Circuits supplying small Edison screw (SES) or small bayonet cap (SBC) fittings must be supplied from Protective Devices of nominal current rating no greater than 10 A. Switching of Circuits containing discharge lighting or other lighting with high inductance may require special consideration; the rating of the switch shall be suited to the conditions expected and shall not be less than twice the load current. The design and construction of lighting signs used for publicity, decorative and general purposes (such as neon signs) shall comply with BS 559. High voltage discharge lighting is to be provided with an emergency isolation switch which must be clearly marked and located in an easily accessible position. Heat resistant flexible cords of 10mm² minimum size shall be used for connection between the ceiling rose and lamp holders for pendant type light fittings. All Luminaires must be connected to Final Circuits using a ceiling rose or other purpose made connection point.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.1.26', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.1', cl: '6.1.20–6.1.26', pg: 60, f: 70, t: 'Emergency light fittings – BS 5266 and 3-hour battery', s: ['Emergency Lighting', 'Lighting'], k: ['Lighting', 'Electrical'], fig: 'emlayout',
  x: 'Lighting Circuits in false ceilings or voids must be installed in conduits or trunking. Outdoor lighting should be of suitable weatherproof construction with appropriate connection points and fittings. Underwater lighting must be supplied by a Separated Extra-Low Voltage System (SELV) not exceeding 12 V a.c. or 30 V d.c. Emergency light fittings must comply with BS 5266 and shall be provided with a battery of minimum 3 hours rating.', a: { duration: '180 min', el_standard: 'BS 5266' },
  e: 'SEWA requires emergency light fittings to comply with BS 5266 and to have a battery rated for at least 3 hours.', rec: 'Specify BS 5266 compliant luminaires with a minimum 3-hour battery, and check lux levels and spacing against the UAE Fire & Life Safety Code chapter 6.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.2.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.2', cl: '6.2.1–6.2.2', pg: 60, f: 70, t: 'Plugs and socket-outlets – types', s: ['Socket Outlets', 'Small Power'], k: ['Power', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'For domestic Electrical Installations, all single-phase plugs and socket-outlets must comply with BS 1363 and be fitted with a switch. The use of any other type of single-phase plugs and socket-outlets, such as BS 546 (3 round pins) is not permitted. For single-phase applications in non-domestic Electrical Installations either BS 1363 or BS 546 type plugs and socket-outlets are permitted. However, BS 546 plugs and socket-outlets must be restricted to specialist applications such as high load Appliances (BS 546 15 A plug) or where it is desirable not to intermix certain Appliances.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.2.3', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.2', cl: '6.2.3 (positions and circuits)', pg: 61, f: 71, t: 'Socket-outlet positions, heights and ring circuits', s: ['Socket Outlets', 'Small Power'], k: ['Power', 'Low Voltage'],
  x: 'Socket outlets for normal use must be positioned at a standard height of 450 mm or above 100 mm above work surfaces. Where required, low level or skirting height may be used (e.g. in offices) at a minimum of 100 mm above the floor level. Socket outlets in kitchens shall be positioned at least 1 meter away from source of water (e.g. sinks, basins, filter units and supply taps). Socket outlets shall not be mounted at locations where they are liable to come into physical contact with fabrics or other material that may catch fire due to transmission of heat. 13 Amp switched socket outlets installed in rooms other than kitchens, shall be connected using the ring main principle, from 4 numbers up to maximum of 8 numbers of socket outlets in one circuit, or one circuit not covering a floor area of greater than 10 m2 whichever is the less. [Note: twin socket considered as 2 numbers.]', a: { socket_height: '450 mm AFFL', ring_sockets: 'Ring: 4–8 sockets (≤ 10 m² floor area); ≤ 3 sockets radial' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.2.10', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.2', cl: '6.2.10 (circuits)', pg: 62, f: 72, t: 'Ring main, radial and spur circuits for socket outlets', s: ['Socket Outlets', 'Small Power', 'Cable Sizing'], k: ['Power', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Each ring main shall be connected to its own circuit in the final distribution board using 4 mm² PVC cable for the phase and neutral shall be protected by 30A MCB. All conductors shall complete the ring of each circuits, including the earth conductor. [Note: 4 numbers of socket outlet and above shall be connected in ring main principle while 3 numbers and below shall be in radial principle. For kitchen with 3 numbers of socket outlet shall be in ring main principle.] One socket outlet of 13 A rating may be connected to a single phase and neutral circuit wired with 4mm² cables and it is protected by a fuse or circuit breaker not exceeding 20 A. Three phase socket outlets shall be connected on individual radial circuits. For outdoor, damp or wet areas socket outlets must be of the weatherproof type (IP 55) and incorporate a 30mA RCD. Each fan coil unit in a split ducted air conditioning system shall be connected to its own 13 Amps un-switched fused spur unit mounted adjacent to the unit. Spur outlet will not be permitted to connect with any ring main circuit and shall be supplied separately with 4mm² phase and neutral conductor.', a: { socket_conductor: '4 mm² (ring main on 30 A MCB; radial on ≤ 20 A)' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.5.16', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.5', cl: '6.5.16–6.6.3', pg: 67, f: 77, t: 'Segregation of categories; cable types for fixed wiring', s: ['Cable'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Category 3 Circuits (fire and emergency lighting) may not be installed in the same conduit or trunking as Category 1 (mains supplied) Circuits or Category 2 (telecommunications) Circuits unless suitable segregation is provided. The use of different Final Circuits in a common conduit or switch drops in shared conduits are not permitted. For fixed wiring within Premises, PVC, rubber or XLPE insulated cables with stranded copper conductors must be used. Solid-core copper or aluminum conductor cables are not permitted. For locations subject to a higher than normal risk of interference or damage, armored cables are recommended. Where the main switch board is supplied directly from the secondary side of the transformer and no cut-out is installed, the maximum length permitted for these cables from transformer to the main switch boards of the consumer shall be 10 meters.', a: { tx_to_mdb_max: '10 m (no cut-out)' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.6.4', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.6', cl: '6.6.4–6.6.10', pg: 68, f: 78, t: 'Cables – parallel cables, fire-resistant cables for safety circuits', s: ['Cable', 'Fire Rated Cables', 'Emergency Lighting', 'Fire Alarm'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Fire Alarm', 'Lighting'],
  x: 'Cables can be connected in parallel (BS 7671 C7.1) but not applicable to: phase conductors having a current carrying capacity up to and including 150A; any earthing conductors; unequal current sharing; unequal length; different construction, material and cross-sectional area. For locations with higher than normal fire risk, either cables must be installed in metal conduit or mineral insulated copper clad (MICC) complying with BS EN 60702 or enhanced fire-resistance cables must be used. In addition, safety Circuits such as fire alarms, emergency lighting and control Circuits, which are required to remain operational in the event of a fire, must be installed in metal conduits or supplied by MICC cables. [Note: enhanced fire-resistance cables should meet the PH 120 classification when tested in accordance with BS EN 50200 and the 120 minutes survival time when tested in accordance with BS 8434-2.]', a: { fire_cable_rating: 'PH 120 / 120 min (BS EN 50200, BS 8434-2) or MICC / metal conduit' },
  e: 'Fire alarm, emergency lighting and control circuits that must keep working in a fire must be in metal conduit or MICC cable; enhanced fire-resistant cables should be PH 120 (BS EN 50200) with 120 minutes survival (BS 8434-2).', rec: 'State PH 120 / 120 min or MICC on the fire alarm and emergency lighting cable schedules and in the specification.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.6.11', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.6', cl: '6.6.11–6.6.17', pg: 69, f: 79, t: 'Cable selection and installation', s: ['Cable', 'Cable Sizing', 'Voltage Drop'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'The cross section of cables shall be selected according to the expected load, voltage drop, ambient temperature and installation conditions using Appendix A1 including appropriate grouping factors. [Note: the sizing of final circuits and circuits feeding FDB’s must be in accordance with the connected load. Other circuits may be sized in accordance with the Diversified Load, with allowance made for future load growth where appropriate.] The use of single core armored cables should be avoided due to the possibility of induced heating effects. Cables running through inaccessible areas such as walls, floors and solid ceilings shall be installed, without exception, in conduits or trunking so as to be withdrawable in the future. Non-sheathed cables must not be installed in concrete ducts.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.6.18', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.6', cl: '6.6.18 (terminations and colours)', pg: 70, f: 80, t: 'Cable terminations, lift shafts and colour identification', s: ['Cable'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'All cables must be installed between purpose-made termination points (switches, junction boxes, Distribution Boards) and be terminated with purpose-made lugs, crimps, screw or other connectors. Joints between such points are strictly prohibited. Armored cables must be terminated using suitable cable glands which incorporate a suitably rated Earth tag. Cables shall not be installed in lift shafts other than those serving lifts functions. For fixed wiring in an Electrical Installation, the phase colors remain as red, yellow and blue whilst neutral remains as black. However, for flexible sheathed cables supplying single-phase Appliances, brown is used for live and blue is used for neutral.', a: { phase_colours: 'Red / Yellow / Blue; neutral black' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.9.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.8–6.9', cl: '6.8.10–6.9.4', pg: 73, f: 83, t: 'Busways and distribution boards – construction', s: ['DB', 'MDB', 'SMDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'The conductors of a busways shall be protected by HRC fuses or by a suitable circuit breaker. The enclosures of busways shall be securely supported at intervals not exceeding 1.8 meters. Busways use is compulsory for high rise buildings consisting of 15 floors or more (starting from ground floor and including parking area). Busways to be protected to a degree of IP55 minimum. The maximum number of floors to be connected to busways should not exceed 12 unless otherwise approved by SEWA. All Distribution Boards must be factory assembled, type-tested and comply with BS EN 61439. Each Distribution Board must have a neutral bar which is mounted on insulators.', a: { busway_ip: 'IP55 minimum; mandatory for ≥ 15 floors; max 12 floors per busway' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.9.5', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.9', cl: '6.9.5–6.9.8', pg: 74, f: 84, t: 'Final distribution boards – earth leakage zones and capacity', s: ['DB', 'Protection', 'RCD'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Each Distribution Board must have an Earth bar. All Final Distribution Boards must be arranged so as to provide for at least two zones of Earth Leakage Protection (e.g. 100mA and 30mA or 30mA and 30mA and 10mA) and to avoid complete disconnection of power to the area being served, in the event of an earth fault: two or more bus bar sections each protected by a RCD [recommended no more than 9 single phase circuits protected by an individual RCD]; one bus bar section with individual RCBO’s fitted on all final circuits; or two or more FDB’s installed together to serve an area. Other than for small 2-storey buildings, each floor of a Premises shall be provided with at least one Final Distribution Board installed in an easily accessible location. [Note: Final Distribution Boards shall be limited to a maximum capacity of 14 ways three phase (or 42 ways single phase) with a maximum of 125A main incomer and Sub Main Distribution Boards shall be limited to 16 ways three phase.]', a: { fdb_capacity: '≤ 14 ways 3-ph (42 ways 1-ph), 125 A incomer; SMDB ≤ 16 ways' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.9.9', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.9', cl: '6.9.9–6.9.15', pg: 75, f: 85, t: 'Distribution boards – single-phase limit, meters, dedicated room', s: ['DB', 'MDB', 'SMDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'Single-phase Final Distribution Boards may be permitted in a Premises where adequate provision is made for balancing the total load at the Electricity Intake between the three phases. [Note: The maximum single phase load allowed for FDB’s is 7 kW, above this value, FDB’s shall be three phase.] The phase and neutral conductors in all Distribution Boards shall be of the same cross sectional area. Neon indicators, voltmeters and ammeters must be provided for Distribution Boards rated at 400A and above and, where reasonably practicable, for other Distribution Boards. All outgoing Circuits of Distribution Boards must be provided only with circuit-breakers (such as CB’s, MCB’s, MCCB’s, RCD’s, RCBO’s) and shall not contain fuses of any kind, except for capacitor banks. All Distribution Boards must be installed in locations easily accessible for inspection, operation and maintenance, preferably at the entrance to the area they serve. Distribution Boards of rating 200A and above shall be installed in a dedicated room. Distribution Boards of rating below 200A may be installed in a cupboard or other suitable enclosure. [Note: Main Distribution Boards of rating 200A and above must be located in a low voltage switch room.]', a: { db_dedicated_room: 'DBs ≥ 200 A in a dedicated room; MDBs ≥ 200 A in LV switch room', fdb_1ph_max: '7 kW single-phase FDB' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.9.16', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.9', cl: '6.9.16–6.9.21', pg: 76, f: 86, t: 'Distribution boards – location, IP rating, spare ways, height, sub-main limit', s: ['DB', 'SMDB', 'MDB'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], fig: 'dbclear',
  x: 'Distribution Boards shall not be installed in location where water is used (e.g. kitchens, bathrooms or toilet, pump rooms and etc.) within 2 meter of any pipe or other source of water or in difficult to access positions (such as stairways). Distribution Boards and all electrical equipment installed outdoors must be corrosion resistant and give protection against mechanical damage and a minimum ingress protection of IP 55. Distribution Boards shall be provided with sufficient number of protective devices so as to enable every outgoing circuit to be connected to an individual protective device and shall include not less than 10% spare protective devices or blank positions. All Final Distribution Boards shall be flush mounted at a height not exceeding 2 meters from the top of the finished floor level. Each Final distribution Board shall only supply final circuits on the same floor as the board is located. The maximum current carrying capacity of any sub main installation shall be 600A three phase for any commercial or industrial complex and 400A three phase for any domestic complex.', a: { db_height: '≤ 2000 mm to top of DB', db_spare: '≥ 10% spare ways', db_water_clear: '≥ 2 m from water pipes/sources', submain_max: '600 A (commercial/industrial), 400 A (domestic)' },
  e: 'SEWA final distribution boards are flush mounted with the top no higher than 2 m, at least 2 m away from water sources, IP55 outdoors, with at least 10% spare ways. Sub-mains are limited to 600 A (commercial/industrial) or 400 A (domestic).', rec: 'Include 10% spare ways on every DB schedule and keep DBs out of wet areas and stairways.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-6.10', d: 'sewa20', ch: '6', sec: '6.10', cl: '6.10.1–6.10.8', pg: 77, f: 87, t: 'Low voltage switchboards', s: ['MDB', 'SMDB', 'Circuit Breaker'], k: ['Low Voltage', 'Electrical'], x: 'LV switchboards must consist of type-tested assemblies which satisfy the requirements of BS EN 61439. Switchboards must be located in a dedicated room to provide for safe access and egress at all times. Switch boards rated above 400A must be provided with instruments for voltage, current, maximum demand and power factor measurement, as well as phase indicating lamps. Where more than one incoming supply cable is provided at the low voltage switch room, these must be connected to separate switchboards (or separate sections of the same switchboard) which have the facility for interconnection through a bus coupler circuit-breaker or switch, interlocked to prevent its closure when both incoming supply cables are live. All bus bars in switchboards must be tinned copper and rigidly supported throughout their length. The neutral and Earth bus bars must run throughout the length of the switchboard. The neutral bus bar must be of the same cross sectional area as the phase bus bars. The main incoming circuit-breaker(s) must be clearly marked and without lock to allow immediate operation in the event of emergency. All main switchboard details shall be submitted to SEWA for approval before the switch board is manufactured.', a: { sb_metering: 'V, A, MD, PF instruments above 400 A' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.1–7.2', cl: '7.1.1–7.2.1', pg: 80, f: 90, t: 'Earthing – general principles and systems of earthing', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical'], fig: 'earthing',
  x: 'Earthing of exposed conductive parts of an electrical installation and of appliances in a premises is required and must provide the following functions of safety: a. Allow the passage of fault current in the event of a live conductor touching an Exposed-Conductive-Part. b. Ensure that the magnitude of fault current is sufficient to operate Protective Devices within 0.4 seconds for all parts of an Electrical Installation. c. Ensure that, in association with Protective Devices, high resistance fault to Earth does not persist so as to cause overheating or fire. Locally Earthed System (TT): The Client/Owner provides a main earth terminal for the electrical installation, which is connected to a sufficient number of local earth electrodes to provide a maximum Earth Resistance measured at the Owner’s Main Earth Terminal no greater than 1 Ohm. SEWA Earthed System (TN-S): SEWA provides a connection to the Owner’s Main Earth Terminal, using the distribution network Earthing system. [Note: Earth Resistance values of less than 10 Ohms may be specified by the designer for purposes such as Functional Earthing or for specialist installations such as petrol stations, telecommunications sites, etc.]', a: { earth_res: '≤ 1 Ω', disconnection_time: '≤ 0.4 s' },
  e: 'A SEWA installation uses a combination of a locally earthed system (TT) with no more than 1 Ω at the Main Earth Terminal and the SEWA-provided TN-S connection; protective devices must clear earth faults within 0.4 seconds.', rec: 'Record the measured earth resistance at the MET on the installation certificate and show a removable link between the local earth and the SEWA earth at the MET.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.2.2', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.2–7.3', cl: '7.2.2–7.3.4', pg: 81, f: 91, t: 'Earthing – approval, neutral/earth separation, main earth terminal', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical'], x: 'The type of earthing system must be stated on the electrical installation certificate and clearly labelled at the Main Distribution Board. SEWA earthing system to be used in Sharjah Emirate is a combination of both TN-S and TT. In all cases, the neutral and earth conductors must be kept separate and not connected together at the MET or at any other point downstream from the Client/Owner’s Connection Point. The Client/Owner’s electrical installation must include a Main Earth Terminal located close to or within the Main Distribution Board and must be clearly labelled. A means of removing the connection to the Locally Earthed System (TT) and the connection to the SEWA’s Earthed System (TN-S) (such as by a removable metal link) must be provided at the Main Earth Terminal to facilitate testing. The Earth Resistance of the Locally Earthed System (TT) measured at the MET shall be recorded on the Electrical Installation Certificate.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.4', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.4', cl: '7.4.1–7.4.8', pg: 82, f: 92, t: 'Earth electrodes', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical'], fig: 'earthing',
  x: 'A Locally Earthed System (TT) shall be provided at all premises. For Premises consisting of more than one building, a Locally Earthed System (TT) must be provided at each building that is at a distance of more than 30 meters from the low voltage switch room. Earth Electrodes will normally comprise of 15 mm diameter copper rods driven to a minimum depth of 3 meters. The top of each Earth Electrode must be housed inside a 300 mm x 300 mm x 300 mm inspection pit which is clearly labelled and accessible for routine testing. Where more than one Earth Electrode is required to achieve the required Earth Resistance value, these must be separated at a distance greater than their mutual resistance zone. [Note: a minimum separation of twice the Earth Electrode depth is considered to give adequate separation of the mutual resistance zones.] For supplies of 500 A rating and above, at least two independent Earth Electrodes must be provided, regardless of the Earth Resistance value achieved for each Earth Electrode, and connected to the same Main Earth Terminal. Metallic service pipes such gas shall not be used as Earth Electrodes.', a: { earth_rod_depth: '3 m', earth_pit: '300 × 300 × 300 mm', multi_electrode: '≥ 2 electrodes for supplies ≥ 500 A', earth_spacing: '≥ 2 × electrode depth between electrodes' },
  e: 'SEWA earth electrodes are normally 15 mm copper rods driven at least 3 m, each in a 300 × 300 × 300 mm inspection pit; supplies of 500 A and above need at least two independent electrodes.', rec: 'Show electrode pits with inspection covers on the earthing layout, space electrodes by at least twice their depth, and provide two electrodes when the incomer is 500 A or more.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.5', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.5–7.6', cl: '7.4.8–7.6.5', pg: 83, f: 93, t: 'Earth conductors and structural electrodes', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection', 'Cable'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical'], x: 'The use of structural foundations or piles as an Earth Electrode shall be in accordance with BS 7430 and subject to: precautions against electrolysis; electrical continuity between all metalwork forming part of the Earth Electrode; provision for measuring and monitoring the Earth Resistance value at regular intervals; prior approval of the design by the SEWA; consent of the Client/Owner; consent of the premises structural engineer. Main Earth Conductors (MEC) are conductors connected between Earth Electrodes and Main Earth Terminals. Circuit Earth Conductors (CEC) connect all Circuits emanating from Main Distribution Boards, Sub Main Distribution Boards, Final Distribution Boards to equipment and Appliances. All Earth Conductors must be covered with green/yellow PVC insulation and be securely terminated with purpose-made lugs or fixings. Where associated with circuits, all earth conductors must be labelled at their termination points with Circuit identification numbers.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.5.7', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.5–7.6', cl: '7.5.7–7.6.5', pg: 84, f: 94, t: 'Circuit earth conductors and exposed-conductive-parts', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical'], x: 'All Circuits must have a Circuit Earth Conductor sized in accordance with clause 7.8.4. Circuit Earth Conductors must run alongside the associated phase and neutral conductors. For metal-sheathed or armored cables, the sheath or armoring shall not be used as the Earth Conductor; however, it shall be terminated by suitable cable glands. The use of metal conduit, trunking, bus bar trunking or switchgear metal enclosures as Earth Conductors is not permitted without the prior approval of the SEWA. No switches, isolators or circuit-breakers may be installed in the electrical path of any Earth Conductor. All exposed conductive parts of an electrical installation, including appliances, must be connected to earth via earth conductors. Class I Appliances must be provided with a suitable connection point or plug and socket arrangement including a connection to the circuit earth conductor. All connection points in an electrical installation must include an earth conductor for future use.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.7', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.7', cl: '7.7.1–7.7.4', pg: 85, f: 95, t: 'Earth fault loop impedance', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection', 'Circuit Breaker'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'The Earth Fault Loop Impedance (Zs) is defined as the total impedance presented to an earth fault current. All Electrical Installations must be designed and tested such that the Earth Fault Loop Impedance at any point in the Electrical Installation is of a sufficient value to operate Protective Devices within 0.4 seconds. The maximum Earth Fault Loop Impedance values required to meet the disconnection times in clause 7.7.2 for MCB devices are given in Appendix A Table 9. The method for measuring earth fault loop impedance is given in Appendix B Figures 50 and 51.', a: { disconnection_time: '≤ 0.4 s' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.8', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.8', cl: '7.8.1–7.8.3', pg: 86, f: 96, t: 'Protective conductors – adiabatic formula and k values', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection', 'Cable Sizing'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'The cross sectional area of protective conductors shall either be calculated in accordance with Section 7.8.2 or selected from the table. The cross sectional area shall not be less than the value determined by the formula (applicable only for disconnecting times not exceeding 5s): S = √(I² · t) / k, where S = cross sectional area in mm², I = value (a.c. r.m.s) of fault current for fault of negligible impedance which can flow through the protective device in amperes, t = operating time of the disconnecting device in seconds. If application of the formula produces non-standard sizes, conductor of the nearest higher standard cross sectional area shall be used. ‘k’ values of insulated protective conductors not incorporated in cables or bare protective conductors with cable covering: copper 143 (PVC, final temperature 160 °C), 176 (EPR/XLPE, 250 °C), 166 (butyl rubber, 220 °C); aluminum 95, 116, 110; steel 52, 64, 60. [Note: initial temperature of the conductor is assumed 20 °C.]', a: { pe_formula: 'S = √(I²·t) / k' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-7.9', d: 'sewa20', ch: '7', sec: '7.8–7.9', cl: '7.8.4–7.9.6', pg: 88, f: 98, t: 'Protective conductor sizing table and equipotential bonding conductors', s: ['Earthing', 'Protection', 'Cable Sizing'], k: ['Earthing', 'Electrical', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Minimum cross sectional area of the protective conductor (SP) for a phase conductor cross sectional area S: S ≤ 16 mm² – SP = S; 16 < S ≤ 35 mm² – SP = 16 mm²; S > 35 mm² – SP = S/2. Main equipotential bonding conductors shall have cross-sectional areas not less than half the cross-sectional area of the largest protective conductor of the installation subject to a minimum of 6 mm². The cross-sectional area need not however, exceed 25 mm² if the bonding conductor is of copper. Supplementary equipotential bonding conductor connecting two exposed conductive parts may have a cross-sectional area not less than that of the smaller protective conductor connected to the exposed conductive parts. Bonding of water meters shall be bonded across.', a: { pe_size: 'S ≤ 16: S; 16–35: 16 mm²; > 35: S/2', main_bonding: '≥ ½ largest PE, min 6 mm², max 25 mm²' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-8.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '8', sec: '8.1', cl: '8.1', pg: 90, f: 100, t: 'Assessment of connected load', s: ['Load Estimation', 'Load Schedule', 'Socket Outlets', 'Motors'], k: ['Electrical', 'Power'], x: 'LED lights and other lightings shall be rated according to the actual rating. A minimum of 200W per point may be considered for each 13A switched socket outlet installed for general utility purpose other than in kitchen. For a cooker, a minimum of 3,000W. A current demand of 15A shall be assumed for each 15A switched socket outlet circuit. However, for general purpose utility socket outlets an assumed load of 1,000W per socket outlet installed in commercial and industrial premises and 500W per socket outlet in residential premises may be permitted. For industrial sockets, stationary appliances and equipment including air conditioning unit, the actual load of each appliances and equipment shall be considered as connected load. The minimum connected load for transfer or booster pumps in a domestic, small office blocks and similar loads shall be 1 Hp (or 746W). For any electrical installations directly connected to SEWA network, future or spare load are allowed but shall not be included in the total connected load, however, future or spare load can be used in addition to size the main incomer circuit breaker and the main incomer cable. [Note: for any electrical installations connected to private transformer, future or spare load shall be included to the total connected load.]', a: { socket_load: '1000 W (commercial) / 500 W (residential) per utility socket' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-8.2', d: 'sewa20', ch: '8', sec: '8.2', cl: '8.2.1–8.2.3', pg: 91, f: 101, t: 'Maximum demand – diversity allowances for SMDB sizing', s: ['Demand Factor', 'Diversity Factor', 'Load Estimation', 'Load Schedule'], k: ['Electrical', 'Power'], x: 'All Distribution boards shall be rated for the total connected load or maximum demand load with the application of diversity factor. The diversity allowances shown in the table below are for specific situations and meant to only provide guidance, used for sizing of the Sub-Main Distribution Boards (SMDB’s) only. Percentages (Residential premises, villas, flats / Shops, stores, offices, schools, mosques, business premises / Hotels, motels, accommodation houses): Lighting 80% / 90% / 90%; 13A socket outlets (general use for portable appliances) 60% / 75% / 60%; 13A socket outlets/flex outlets (fixed appliances) 60% / 60% / 60%; Water heater 60% / 60% / 60%; Washing machine, dryer, dishwasher 60% / 60% / 60%; Cooker 60% / 80% / 85%; Motors 100% / 100% / 100%; Air-conditioning 100% / 100% / 100%. [Note: No diversity allowance is allowed for centralized water heating.]', a: { diversity_lighting: '80% residential / 90% commercial & hotels' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-8.2.4', d: 'sewa20', ch: '8', sec: '8.2', cl: '8.2.4–8.2.5', pg: 92, f: 102, t: 'Maximum demand at the main distribution board', s: ['Diversity Factor', 'Demand Factor', 'MDB'], k: ['Electrical'], x: 'An appropriate diversity factor shall be permitted to determine the maximum demand at the main distribution level. The following diversity factor recommended by SEWA to be applied in the main distribution circuit for Main Distribution Boards (MDB’s) are: Residential and similar – 80% diversity factor; Commercial and similar – 90% diversity factor; Industrial and similar – 100% diversity factor. The design method and the proposed diversity factor shall be clearly indicated in the design drawing and load distribution schedules submitted for SEWA’s approval.', a: { mdb_diversity: '80% residential / 90% commercial / 100% industrial' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-10.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '10', sec: '10.1', cl: '10.1.1–10.1.9', pg: 100, f: 110, t: 'Power factor correction – general requirements', s: ['Power Factor'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'The power factor at any Connection Point between the Distribution Company (SEWA) and Client/Owner’s Electrical Installation shall be maintained between 0.95 lagging and unity. Air-conditioning units, motors, large electrical machines, fluorescent or discharge lighting and etc. shall be provided with power factor correction. Notwithstanding the above, the use of capacitor banks in residential villas shall not be permitted. Any electrical installations with transformer and an electrical installations with 80 kW and above load shall be provided with capacitor banks. To minimize harmonic risk, harmonic filter reactors must be employed in series with capacitors. For induction motors with a permanently connected capacitor unit, the capacitor unit rating must not exceed 90% of the no-load reactive power of the motor. Capacitors shall normally be of dry type, metal encapsulated, sealed type; the use of oil containing PCB is strictly prohibited. Power factor correction capacitors shall be provided with a means of prompt discharge on disconnection. Capacitor banks and associated components shall be suitably designed for a maximum system voltage of 440 V and a maximum ambient temperature of 55 °C.', a: { pf: '0.95 lagging to unity' },
  e: 'SEWA requires the power factor at the connection point to be maintained between 0.95 lagging and unity; installations with a transformer or 80 kW and above need capacitor banks (not permitted in residential villas), with harmonic filter reactors in series.', rec: 'Provide an automatically regulated capacitor bank with detuning reactors, designed for 440 V and 55 °C, in a separate cubicle.' },
{ id: 'sewa20-10.1.10', d: 'sewa20', ch: '10', sec: '10.1–10.2', cl: '10.1.10–10.2.4', pg: 101, f: 111, t: 'Capacitor banks – cubicle, specification, discharge', s: ['Power Factor'], k: ['Electrical', 'Low Voltage'], x: 'Capacitor bank shall not be a part of the motor control center, main LV panel or sub-main panel but it shall be accommodated in a separate cubicle. All equipment and components of the capacitor banks shall be capable of continuous operation at their full current and voltage rating at system continuous deviation of up to and including: Voltage ± 10%, Frequency ± 5%. Components (MCCB/ACB/Isolator, fuses, bus bar and power cables) shall be oversized and based on 1.5 times the capacitor rated current. Capacitor units shall be capable of continuous operation in accordance with IEC 60831. Built-in discharge resistors for capacitors shall be sized to ensure safe discharge of the capacitor to less than 50 V in one minute after a switch off. [Note: capacitors should not restart until a minimum 3 minutes after the restoration of the supply.]', a: { cap_components: '1.5 × capacitor rated current; discharge < 50 V in 1 min' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-11.1', d: 'sewa20', ch: '11', sec: '11.1', cl: '11.1.1–11.1.8', pg: 106, f: 116, t: 'Standby generator – general requirements', s: ['Generator'], k: ['Generator', 'Electrical'], fig: 'genroom',
  x: 'Installation and connection of standby generators in any Electrical Installation, for the purpose of maintaining power in the case of a failure of the incoming supply, shall be permitted only with the prior approval of SEWA. The changeover circuit-breaker (ATS) shall have 4 poles for a three-phase generator and 2 poles for a single-phase generator, to ensure that all live and neutral conductors are disconnected at the same time. The installation and changeover arrangements must ensure that there is no possibility of paralleling the generator and incoming mains supply. If paralleling arrangements are required, these must be mechanically and electrically interlocked and be specifically assessed and approved by SEWA. Standby generator shall not be installed in basement except those design with the access of ventilation for the generator and on roof of the building in general, exception for special and sensitive electrical installation shall be permitted only with prior approval by SEWA. Standby generator shall be provided for a multi-storey building (from G+5 and above) and other electrical installations that needed electrical supply in an event of emergency. The requirements for generator connections and Earthing arrangements shall comply with the following standards (BS 4999, BS 5000, BS 5514, IEC 60034, NEMA MG 1.22, ISO 8528 and VDE 0530). The full details of the equipment, circuit and wiring diagram, essential loads, generator room and etc. shall be submitted to SEWA for approval before the commencement of work.', a: { gen_changeover: '4-pole ATS (3-ph); no paralleling unless approved', gen_required: 'Required for multi-storey buildings G+5 and above' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-11.2', d: 'sewa20', ch: '11', sec: '11.2', cl: '11.2.1–11.2.6', pg: 107, f: 117, t: 'Distribution transformer – general requirements and ratings', s: ['Transformer'], k: ['Transformer', 'Medium Voltage'], x: 'The transformers shall be of the naturally cooled, outdoor, oil immersed and hermetically sealed core type. The transformers shall conform to the latest edition of IEC 60076, B.S. 171 or any equivalent international standard. Where the transformers shall be installed outdoors in U.A.E salt laden dusty atmosphere where the maximum humidity of 100% and maximum ambient temperature of 50-55 °C can be experienced, the transformer shall be suitable for continuous operation at the above site conditions. The nominal primary voltage is 11 or 6.6 kV, 50 Hz; the symmetrical fault level of the HT network is 25 kA; the secondary voltage at full load shall be 415 Volts, 50 Hz, neutral point shall be earthed. The approved ratings of distribution transformer used in Sharjah are: 100 kVA (Pole Mounted); 200 kVA (Pole Mounted); 250 kVA (Pad Mounted); 500 kVA (Pad Mounted); 1,000 kVA (Pad Mounted); 1,500 kVA (Pad Mounted); 2,000 kVA (Pad Mounted) – needs approval from SEWA prior of proposal design.', a: { tx_ratings: '100, 200 (pole); 250, 500, 1000, 1500 kVA (pad); 2000 kVA by approval', mv_fault: '25 kA (HT network)' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-11.2.7', d: 'sewa20', ch: '11', sec: '11.2', cl: '11.2.6–11.2.10', pg: 108, f: 118, t: 'Distribution transformer – LV cable boxes and permitted connected load', s: ['Transformer', 'Load Estimation'], k: ['Transformer', 'Electrical'], x: 'The L.V. cable box with glands shall be suitable for the following sizes of cable: 14 single core 630 mm² cables for 2,000 kVA transformer; 11 single core 630 mm² for 1,500 kVA; 7 single core 630 mm² for 1,000 kVA; 4 single core 630 mm² for 500 kVA; single 4 core 300 mm² for 250 kVA; single 4 core 185 mm² for 200 kVA; single 4 core 70 mm² for 100 kVA. Total connected load permitted loading for transformer: Residential villas – 1,500 kVA: 1300 kW; 1,000 kVA: 860 kW; 500 kVA: 450 kW; 250 kVA: 220 kW. Commercial/residential building, malls, schools, mosques – 1300 kW; 860 kW; 450 kW; 220 kW. Farms – 1,200; 800; 400; 200; 160 (200 kVA); 80 kW (100 kVA). Industrial areas – 1,200; 800; 400; 200 kW. [Note: for any motor/compressor loads exceeding 100 kW the transformer shall be normally limited to: 1,500 kVA Transformer – 950 kW; 1,000 kVA Transformer – 650 kW.] Sub main cables and final circuit wiring shall not be permitted to pass through sub-station/transformer and MV rooms.', a: { tx_1000_md: '860 kW (1,000 kVA, villas/commercial); 650 kW with motor loads' } },
{ id: 'sewa20-12.8', d: 'sewa20', ch: '12', sec: '12.8', cl: '12.8.1–12.8.2', pg: 116, f: 126, t: 'External lighting', s: ['Outdoor Lighting', 'Landscape Lighting', 'Lighting', 'Protection', 'RCD'], k: ['Lighting', 'Electrical'], x: 'External lighting luminaires mounted on poles shall be supplied from a suitably rated single phase RCBO. The connecting cable between the Luminaire and the cutout shall be a 3 core (L-N-E) Double Insulated heat resistant flexible cord. The cut-out shall be metal enclosed or ABS plastic with minimum ingress protection of IP55. Temporary supplies taken from external lighting cutouts, such as for decorative lighting or signboards, must be provided with Earth Leakage Protection using RCBOs of residual current rating preferably 10mA but no greater than 30 mA. All Circuits supplying external lighting shall be single phase and protected by an Earth Leakage Protected System and by an Earthed Equipotential Bonded System. [Note: the use of three phase supply arrangements must be approved by SEWA.] Looping of external lighting Circuits shall be via purpose made junction boxes or cut-outs.' }
);
